   "text": {
    "L1": "A letter in an expression stands for a number, or amount.",
    "L2": "A common myth says a letter gets its value from its place in the alphabet. That is not true.",
    "L3": "For example, n can mean the number of pencils. It does not mean one pencil as an object.",
    "L4": "If each pencil costs five rupees, the cost of n pencils is 5n, which means 5 times n.",
    "L5": "Be careful: 5n multiplies by five, while n plus 5 adds five. They do not mean the same thing.",
    "L6": "For n samosas at twelve rupees each and one ten-rupee chai, write 12n plus 10. When n is 4, the cost is 58 rupees.",
    "L7": "A letter can have a different value in another question, and different letters can share a value. The expression still uses one value for each repeated letter.",
    "L8": "Your turn: choose the expression for n samosas at twelve rupees each and one ten-rupee chai."
   },
